{{-- Breadcrumbs for the marketing pages, on the shared <Breadcrumb>.

     Reads two things from the including page: `breadcrumbs`, an array of
     `{ label, href? }` with the deepest last, and `breadcrumbWidth`, the
     container width of that page's own content. The last entry is the current
     page and carries no link, because a link to where you already are is a link
     nobody wants. That array shape is the component's `items` shape, so it
     passes straight through.

     The width goes through className, which lands on the <nav>. A width
     competes with nothing the component sets, so the append-order caveat in
     stacksjs/stx#1993 does not bite here. It is passed in rather than fixed
     because the prose pages are narrower than the card grids, and a trail that
     does not start above the heading it belongs to reads as a stray line.

     This was hand-written until now for a reason that is gone: stx used to drop
     props passed component to component, so shared chrome built as a component
     rendered empty. Fixed as of 0.2.395.

     What was left was a styling difference, and it is deliberate rather than
     incidental. The component paints separators with `fg-soft` and links with
     `fg-muted`; this file used to paint the separator `text-line`, the hairline
     --border, which measured 1.23:1 against the page in light and 1.18:1 in
     dark. A divider is allowed to be seen. `fg-soft` is --text-3 here (it used
     to be --text-2, the same value as fg-muted, which would have made the
     separator exactly as loud as the labels it divides - fixed separately), so
     it lands at 4.72:1 and 5.36:1. The current page also gains font-medium.

     `list-none` and `no-underline` are not passed on: measured on the served
     page, the component's own markup already computes list-style-type: none,
     padding-left: 0 and text-decoration-line: none, so repeating them would be
     cargo. --}}
<Breadcrumb className="px-6 pt-8 mx-auto {{ breadcrumbWidth }}" :items="breadcrumbs" />
